WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.527
Welcome to Day 5 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:04.877 --> 00:00:08.418
Up to now, most of our work has happened outside the courthouse.

00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:10.614
Today the case goes inside it.

00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:13.848
When a claim can't be resolved through negotiation,

00:00:13.848 --> 00:00:17.581
it becomes a lawsuit, and your job changes in some important ways.

00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:20.704
Deadlines get stricter, documents become evidence,

00:00:20.704 --> 00:00:23.144
and the client needs more support than ever.

00:00:23.494 --> 00:00:26.537
Our sample case is still John Doe versus Robert Smith,

00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:29.241
the same file you're working on in the platform.

00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:34.000
Today we follow its litigation road, the one where Keystone wouldn't move and we

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.607
filed suit.

00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:39.434
We'll walk through litigation step by step, spend real time on getting John ready

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:43.525
for his deposition, and build out your toolkit of documents and deadlines.

00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:48.031
Then we'll look at how property damage is valued, using Margaret Doe's Civic.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:49.600
Let's get started.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.973
Here's the road map for today.

00:00:54.323 --> 00:00:57.737
The first and biggest block is the litigation phase itself,

00:00:57.737 --> 00:01:01.556
meaning what happens once a lawsuit is filed and where you fit in.

00:01:01.906 --> 00:01:05.490
The second block takes a topic you already know from pre-lit,

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.719
property damage, and asks how it plays out once a case is in litigation.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:14.284
The third item on the agenda is liability disputes and MIA clients.

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:17.269
On our file, you'll see both along the way.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:22.285
The liability dispute is defense counsel's claim that John shares the blame for the

00:01:22.285 --> 00:01:26.726
crash, and the MIA client is the stretch when John himself couldn't be reached,

00:01:26.726 --> 00:01:28.300
right before his deposition.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.482
Keep that list in mind, because each part builds on the one before it.

00:01:35.032 --> 00:01:39.513
So what actually changes when negotiations break down and we file suit?

00:01:39.863 --> 00:01:41.906
Look at how it happened on our file.

00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:47.231
Keystone held at its offer, the Time-Limited Demand lapsed on August 21st,

00:01:47.231 --> 00:01:52.878
2026, and while the BI claim sat unresolved, the whole UM stack behind it was frozen

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:53.147
too.

00:01:53.497 --> 00:01:57.305
Attorney Thorne authorized suit, and on September 10th,

00:01:57.305 --> 00:02:01.252
2026, the file's status in the CMS changed to Litigation.

00:02:01.602 --> 00:02:04.424
From that point on, we're dealing with a court,

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:07.545
not an adjuster, and the court runs on a structured,

00:02:07.545 --> 00:02:08.626
mandatory process.

00:02:08.976 --> 00:02:13.411
Miss a deadline or misfile a document, and the case can be thrown out entirely.

00:02:13.761 --> 00:02:16.524
Notice the point about property damage, too.

00:02:16.874 --> 00:02:21.286
PD can prove the client was really hurt, or it can become a defense weapon.

00:02:21.636 --> 00:02:26.248
On our file, the PD is Margaret's Civic, a separate claim we'll come back to at the

00:02:26.248 --> 00:02:27.081
end of the day.

00:02:27.431 --> 00:02:31.216
Now the lifecycle: pleadings, then discovery, then trial prep.

00:02:31.566 --> 00:02:36.091
You track service and draft the first files, then gather discovery and prepare the

00:02:36.091 --> 00:02:39.844
client, then coordinate schedules and bring the medicals up to date.

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:45.586
Phase one is about getting the lawsuit started the right way.

00:02:45.936 --> 00:02:50.580
Think of it as two jobs: file on time, and make sure the defendant is truly

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.138
notified.

00:02:51.488 --> 00:02:54.646
The filing piece comes down to the Statute of Limitations.

00:02:54.995 --> 00:02:59.013
On our file, the SOL is docketed for February 6th,

00:02:59.013 --> 00:03:05.764
2028, and because that's a Sunday, the firm sets its own hard date two days earlier,

00:03:05.764 --> 00:03:07.290
February 4th, 2028.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.482
We filed on September 24th, 2026, well ahead of either date.

00:03:12.832 --> 00:03:15.386
That's the habit to build: never file at the edge.

00:03:15.736 --> 00:03:17.187
Next comes service.

00:03:17.537 --> 00:03:21.379
Once the Complaint is filed, the defendant has to be personally served,

00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:23.435
and you coordinate the process server.

00:03:23.785 --> 00:03:27.094
For Robert Smith, that was Allied Process Services.

00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:28.616
Then close the loop.

00:03:28.966 --> 00:03:31.846
Get the signed Affidavit of Service back and filed.

00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:39.115
Smith was served on October 14th, and the affidavit was filed on October 16th, 2026.

00:03:39.465 --> 00:03:42.890
That document is what stops a defendant from claiming he never knew.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.549
And if a defendant is dodging service, don't sit on it.

00:03:46.899 --> 00:03:50.811
Flag it for your attorney, who can ask the court for alternative service.

00:03:53.361 --> 00:03:56.204
Discovery is where each side has to show its cards,

00:03:56.204 --> 00:03:59.382
and it's where you'll spend most of your litigation time,

00:03:59.382 --> 00:04:00.664
about 80 percent of it.

00:04:01.014 --> 00:04:03.394
It starts with paperwork from the defense.

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.020
Interrogatories are written questions.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:09.632
Requests for Production are demands for actual documents.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:13.555
On our file, that packet arrived on December 1st,

00:04:13.555 --> 00:04:17.420
2026: 25 interrogatories, 30 Requests for Production,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.650
and 15 Requests for Admission, all served on John by mail.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.254
Here's the big lesson.

00:04:23.604 --> 00:04:27.563
It's tempting to forward a packet like that to John and ask him to fill it out.

00:04:27.913 --> 00:04:28.563
Don't.

00:04:28.913 --> 00:04:33.452
Clients get these wrong, leave things blank, or answer in ways that hurt them.

00:04:33.802 --> 00:04:38.412
Instead, set up a dedicated Discovery Intake Meeting, in person or on Zoom.

00:04:38.762 --> 00:04:42.784
Go through every question together, put the answers into the formal template,

00:04:42.784 --> 00:04:45.240
and gather the documents the defense asked for.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.922
Finally, the client signs a Verification Page under oath.

00:04:49.272 --> 00:04:52.884
That signature means the answers follow John for the rest of the case,

00:04:52.884 --> 00:04:55.774
which is exactly why we take the time to get them right.

00:04:58.324 --> 00:05:00.971
The other half of discovery is the deposition.

00:05:01.321 --> 00:05:05.427
This is an oral interview under oath, with a court reporter taking down every word,

00:05:05.427 --> 00:05:09.582
and the defense attorney may question your client for hours looking for something to

00:05:09.582 --> 00:05:10.225
trip them up.

00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:14.209
On our file, the notice went out on January 20th,

00:05:14.209 --> 00:05:17.916
2027, setting John's deposition for February 18th,

00:05:17.916 --> 00:05:21.401
2027, at 10 in the morning, by videoconference,

00:05:21.401 --> 00:05:23.625
with Riverton Court Reporting.

00:05:23.975 --> 00:05:26.204
Your work here falls into two parts.

00:05:26.554 --> 00:05:27.889
The first is logistics.

00:05:28.239 --> 00:05:32.895
The slide mentions the court reporter, a videographer, and a room or a Zoom link.

00:05:33.245 --> 00:05:37.048
For John, it's a video deposition, so the technology has to work,

00:05:37.048 --> 00:05:40.090
and the calendars of your attorney, defense counsel,

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.138
and the client all have to line up.

00:05:42.488 --> 00:05:44.566
The second part is the human side.

00:05:44.916 --> 00:05:47.331
Most clients are scared of depositions.

00:05:47.681 --> 00:05:52.360
So a week ahead, send a preparation packet, and keep the basics in front of them:

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.479
listen carefully, pause before answering, never guess,

00:05:55.479 --> 00:05:56.981
and always tell the truth.

00:05:57.331 --> 00:06:00.558
We'll go much deeper on deposition prep in a few slides.

00:06:03.108 --> 00:06:07.714
As the case gets close to mediation or trial, your job is to hand your attorney a

00:06:07.714 --> 00:06:08.681
complete picture.

00:06:09.031 --> 00:06:13.591
On our file, the court ordered mediation, and it's set for March 18th,

00:06:13.591 --> 00:06:17.825
2027, with the mediation statement and binder due a week earlier,

00:06:17.825 --> 00:06:18.737
on March 11th.

00:06:19.087 --> 00:06:20.341
Start with the medicals.

00:06:20.691 --> 00:06:25.675
Litigation can run anywhere from 1 to 3 years, and a lot can change for a client in

00:06:25.675 --> 00:06:26.275
that time.

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:29.260
New doctors, new procedures, new bills.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.922
If the file still shows only the treatment from when we filed,

00:06:32.922 --> 00:06:34.951
the jury sees only part of the injury.

00:06:35.301 --> 00:06:38.587
So do a final run and bring every record and bill up to date.

00:06:38.937 --> 00:06:40.458
Next, subpoenas.

00:06:40.807 --> 00:06:45.403
Witnesses, treating physicians, and police officers don't appear just because we

00:06:45.403 --> 00:06:46.264
hope they will.

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:50.921
A trial subpoena makes it a legal requirement, and you draft and issue them.

00:06:51.271 --> 00:06:52.665
Finally, the binders.

00:06:53.015 --> 00:06:57.540
Every exhibit, from medical records to scene photos to property damage estimates,

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.339
should be indexed and tabbed so your attorney can find it instantly.

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:04.708
Whether it's the mediation binder or a trial binder,

00:07:04.708 --> 00:07:07.030
nobody has time to go looking for paper.

00:07:09.579 --> 00:07:12.912
Moving into litigation also means changing some habits.

00:07:13.262 --> 00:07:15.433
Two traps catch new case managers.

00:07:15.783 --> 00:07:18.964
The first is treating a court deadline like an adjuster's deadline.

00:07:19.314 --> 00:07:22.541
With an adjuster, a polite request often buys you time.

00:07:22.891 --> 00:07:24.586
A court doesn't work that way.

00:07:24.936 --> 00:07:29.525
Miss a discovery deadline, and the judge can bar the client from using key medical

00:07:29.525 --> 00:07:30.532
evidence at trial.

00:07:30.882 --> 00:07:34.284
The second trap is letting the client keep talking to the adjuster.

00:07:34.634 --> 00:07:38.802
Once suit is filed, every contact with the defense goes through the lawyers.

00:07:39.152 --> 00:07:44.422
On our file, Keystone hired defense counsel, Attorney Grant Mercer of Mercer and

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:45.212
Hale, L L P.

00:07:45.562 --> 00:07:49.511
If a Keystone adjuster or a defense investigator reaches out to John,

00:07:49.511 --> 00:07:51.971
step in, stop it, and tell Attorney Thorne.

00:07:52.321 --> 00:07:56.629
To stay on top of it all, audit your files weekly against three measures:

00:07:56.629 --> 00:07:59.757
a process server confirmed within 48 hours of filing,

00:07:59.757 --> 00:08:04.183
draft discovery responses to your attorney at least 7 days before the court

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:08.668
deadline, and a call to every litigation client at least once every 30 days.

00:08:09.018 --> 00:08:10.643
That last one matters.

00:08:10.993 --> 00:08:13.770
A client who feels forgotten is a client who drifts,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.868
and you'll see later today how fast a client can go quiet.

00:08:19.418 --> 00:08:24.147
Let's go deeper on depositions, because a client's testimony can shape the outcome

00:08:24.147 --> 00:08:24.840
of the case.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:28.452
Before any prep meeting, the file gets a careful audit.

00:08:28.802 --> 00:08:33.018
What you're hunting for are landmines, anything the defense can use to make the

00:08:33.018 --> 00:08:35.687
client look dishonest, so counsel sees them first.

00:08:36.037 --> 00:08:37.952
Start with prior medical history.

00:08:38.302 --> 00:08:41.774
The slide's example is a back injury from 2018.

00:08:42.124 --> 00:08:47.749
On our file, it's real: John had three chiropractic visits in 2021 for low-back

00:08:47.749 --> 00:08:49.600
stiffness, which resolved.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.880
The defense will ask about them, and John's answers have to be open and accurate so

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.396
it never looks like he hid them.

00:08:55.746 --> 00:08:59.925
Next, re-read the interrogatory answers the client already signed.

00:09:00.275 --> 00:09:04.702
What John says out loud has to match what he swore to in writing in his discovery

00:09:04.702 --> 00:09:08.309
responses, because consistency is what makes a witness believable.

00:09:08.659 --> 00:09:11.155
And check the client's public social media.

00:09:11.505 --> 00:09:15.810
If a client claims a serious injury but posts photos of a hike or a night of

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:18.529
dancing, your attorney needs to know right away.

00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:23.686
Your job is to find these things and flag them, not to decide what to do about them.

00:09:26.236 --> 00:09:29.963
Once the audit is done, the client comes in, or joins by video,

00:09:29.963 --> 00:09:31.205
for the prep meeting.

00:09:31.555 --> 00:09:34.643
The slide organizes that meeting around three pillars.

00:09:34.993 --> 00:09:39.310
On our file, Attorney Thorne leads the deposition prep sessions,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:43.828
on February 4th and February 16th, 2027, and you run the logistics,

00:09:43.828 --> 00:09:46.661
the technology check and the document set.

00:09:47.011 --> 00:09:49.705
Keep that division in mind as we go through the pillars.

00:09:50.055 --> 00:09:52.481
The first pillar is calming the client down.

00:09:52.831 --> 00:09:54.689
Why does that matter so much?

00:09:55.039 --> 00:09:58.952
Because most people's picture of the legal system comes from television,

00:09:58.952 --> 00:10:03.409
with angry judges and shouting lawyers, and a nervous witness makes more mistakes.

00:10:03.759 --> 00:10:06.267
The slide's script uses a general name.

00:10:06.617 --> 00:10:11.958
For our client, it goes like this: Mr. Doe, this is not a courtroom drama.

00:10:12.308 --> 00:10:15.024
There's no judge, no jury, and no shouting.

00:10:15.374 --> 00:10:18.817
It's a business meeting where the other side hears your story,

00:10:18.817 --> 00:10:21.261
and for John, that meeting happens by video.

00:10:21.611 --> 00:10:25.387
And the line that tends to land best: you can't lose your case today,

00:10:25.387 --> 00:10:27.741
but you can protect it by staying composed.

00:10:30.291 --> 00:10:32.948
The second pillar is the core of deposition prep:

00:10:32.948 --> 00:10:36.147
practicing three testifying habits until they're automatic,

00:10:36.147 --> 00:10:39.671
with mock questions to check whether the client really does them.

00:10:40.021 --> 00:10:43.052
On our file, that practice belongs to Attorney Thorne.

00:10:43.402 --> 00:10:45.909
The case manager never coaches testimony.

00:10:46.259 --> 00:10:50.303
What you need is to understand the habits, so you can support the sessions and

00:10:50.303 --> 00:10:51.495
recognize the problems.

00:10:51.845 --> 00:10:54.342
The first habit is the two-second pause.

00:10:54.692 --> 00:10:59.359
After every question, the client silently counts one, two, then answers.

00:10:59.709 --> 00:11:04.271
That breaks the defense attorney's rhythm and gives our attorney a moment to object.

00:11:04.621 --> 00:11:07.106
The second is understanding the liar's trap.

00:11:07.456 --> 00:11:12.020
When the defense asks about prior injuries, they usually already know the answer

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.447
from insurance databases.

00:11:13.797 --> 00:11:15.201
They're testing honesty.

00:11:15.551 --> 00:11:20.764
For John, that's exactly why the 2021 low-back visits get handled openly.

00:11:21.114 --> 00:11:22.809
The third is to stop talking.

00:11:23.159 --> 00:11:25.121
Short, literal answers only.

00:11:25.471 --> 00:11:29.644
Asked whether he knows what time it was, the answer is yes or no,

00:11:29.644 --> 00:11:30.928
not the time itself.

00:11:31.278 --> 00:11:33.832
Each habit protects the client's credibility.

00:11:36.382 --> 00:11:39.701
The third pillar is how the client looks and carries themselves,

00:11:39.701 --> 00:11:41.723
and it matters more than people expect.

00:11:42.073 --> 00:11:46.181
After the deposition, the defense attorney writes an Evaluation Report on the

00:11:46.181 --> 00:11:46.554
client.

00:11:46.904 --> 00:11:51.090
Think of it as a review of John as a witness, and the insurance company leans on it

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.057
when it decides what the case is worth.

00:11:53.407 --> 00:11:57.146
On our file, that report would come from Attorney Mercer to Keystone.

00:11:57.496 --> 00:11:58.889
The guidance is simple.

00:11:59.239 --> 00:12:04.417
Dress as you would for a job interview or a funeral: neat, clean, and respectful.

00:12:04.767 --> 00:12:09.678
And John's deposition is by videoconference, so he's still on camera the whole time.

00:12:10.028 --> 00:12:12.199
How he presents himself still counts.

00:12:12.549 --> 00:12:15.856
Be clear about what to leave out, too: flashy jewelry,

00:12:15.856 --> 00:12:19.898
expensive watches, and clothing with political or graphic slogans.

00:12:20.248 --> 00:12:21.154
Here's why.

00:12:21.504 --> 00:12:24.416
If a client describes money trouble or serious pain,

00:12:24.416 --> 00:12:28.562
and shows up in designer labels, the story and the appearance don't match,

00:12:28.562 --> 00:12:30.130
and the defense will notice.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:34.955
Now two traps the defense likes to set.

00:12:35.305 --> 00:12:38.463
The slide frames them as mock drills for the prep session.

00:12:38.813 --> 00:12:42.970
On our file, Attorney Thorne runs those, and your job is to understand them.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.084
The first is the is-that-all trap.

00:12:45.434 --> 00:12:46.607
It sounds harmless.

00:12:46.957 --> 00:12:50.130
The defense attorney lists an injury or two and asks,

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:52.286
so you only hurt your neck, correct?

00:12:52.636 --> 00:12:55.051
A nervous client says yes just to agree.

00:12:55.401 --> 00:12:59.764
But that yes can lock them in, and later they can be barred from raising anything

00:12:59.764 --> 00:13:00.033
else.

00:13:00.383 --> 00:13:02.775
Think about how that could land on our file.

00:13:03.125 --> 00:13:08.453
John's main injury is his neck, but his care also documented numbness into his right

00:13:08.453 --> 00:13:11.751
hand, and he had residual right-arm symptoms at MMI.

00:13:12.101 --> 00:13:13.959
A quick yes could leave that out.

00:13:14.308 --> 00:13:17.377
The better answer is honest and keeps the record open,

00:13:17.377 --> 00:13:21.467
something like: these are the main injuries that come to mind right now,

00:13:21.467 --> 00:13:26.069
but my complete medical records detail everything I've discussed with my doctors.

00:13:28.619 --> 00:13:30.454
The second drill is about silence.

00:13:30.804 --> 00:13:35.261
After the client answers, the defense attorney simply says nothing and lets the

00:13:35.261 --> 00:13:36.051
quiet drag on.

00:13:36.401 --> 00:13:37.040
Why?

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:40.022
Because most people can't stand an awkward pause,

00:13:40.022 --> 00:13:42.278
and they start filling it with more words.

00:13:42.628 --> 00:13:46.343
Those extra words are often exactly what the defense was hoping for.

00:13:46.693 --> 00:13:49.971
Picture John finishing a short answer about his gap in treatment,

00:13:49.971 --> 00:13:51.232
and then hearing nothing.

00:13:51.582 --> 00:13:55.927
The urge to keep explaining is strong, and every extra sentence is something new for

00:13:55.927 --> 00:13:57.376
Attorney Mercer to question.

00:13:57.726 --> 00:13:59.386
So the habit is the opposite.

00:13:59.736 --> 00:14:03.560
Once he's answered, he looks at the attorney, or in John's case,

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.053
at the camera, and waits.

00:14:05.403 --> 00:14:09.094
The phrase on the slide says it well: embrace the awkwardness,

00:14:09.094 --> 00:14:10.999
that's their problem, not yours.

00:14:11.349 --> 00:14:15.877
This one feels strange the first time, which is why it gets practiced out loud.

00:14:18.427 --> 00:14:21.437
The day before the deposition is about removing surprises,

00:14:21.437 --> 00:14:23.512
and there are three things to lock down.

00:14:23.862 --> 00:14:25.244
First, logistics.

00:14:25.594 --> 00:14:29.467
Confirm the date, the time and the video link with everyone involved:

00:14:29.467 --> 00:14:32.386
John, Attorney Thorne, and Riverton Court Reporting.

00:14:32.736 --> 00:14:34.106
Second, exhibits.

00:14:34.456 --> 00:14:37.312
Your attorney should have the final medical records,

00:14:37.312 --> 00:14:39.564
invoices, and photos organized and ready.

00:14:39.914 --> 00:14:42.584
Third, a backup way to reach the client.

00:14:42.934 --> 00:14:47.700
This is where our file shows why it matters, and it's the MIA client from today's

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:48.112
agenda.

00:14:48.462 --> 00:14:54.047
From January 22nd to February 3rd, 2027, John went unreachable.

00:14:54.397 --> 00:14:56.614
He'd lost his phone and had a new number.

00:14:56.964 --> 00:15:02.047
The No Contact Protocol ran, and he was finally reached through his mother,

00:15:02.047 --> 00:15:05.368
Margaret, his emergency contact, on February 3rd,

00:15:05.368 --> 00:15:05.706
2027.

00:15:06.056 --> 00:15:09.675
That was just before the first prep session and about two weeks before the

00:15:09.675 --> 00:15:10.213
deposition.

00:15:10.563 --> 00:15:14.690
So the day before, make sure you have the client's current number and a working

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:15.056
backup.

00:15:15.406 --> 00:15:19.757
None of this is complicated, but skipping it is how good preparation falls apart at

00:15:19.757 --> 00:15:20.595
the last minute.

00:15:23.145 --> 00:15:25.974
This is probably the first question every client asks:

00:15:25.974 --> 00:15:27.546
what are they going to ask me?

00:15:27.895 --> 00:15:30.287
Clients feel calmer when they know what's coming.

00:15:30.637 --> 00:15:33.299
The defense can ask about a broad range of things,

00:15:33.299 --> 00:15:35.908
but the questions usually fall into four buckets.

00:15:36.258 --> 00:15:39.353
The first is personal background, like work history,

00:15:39.353 --> 00:15:41.912
where they've lived, and any past lawsuits.

00:15:42.262 --> 00:15:45.574
The second is how the crash happened, meaning the lane,

00:15:45.574 --> 00:15:49.367
the speed, the weather, what they saw, and how the impact felt.

00:15:49.717 --> 00:15:53.154
The third is medical history, before and after the accident.

00:15:53.504 --> 00:15:57.788
The fourth is how the injury has changed daily life, work, hobbies and family.

00:15:58.138 --> 00:15:59.624
Now look at our file.

00:15:59.974 --> 00:16:03.690
Attorney Mercer's questions to John focused on four things:

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:07.657
the gap before his first treatment, the disruption in his care,

00:16:07.657 --> 00:16:10.365
the 2021 low-back visits, and the seatbelt.

00:16:10.715 --> 00:16:14.946
You can see how those map onto the buckets: the seatbelt falls under the crash

00:16:14.946 --> 00:16:17.658
itself, and the other three under medical history.

00:16:18.008 --> 00:16:22.169
None of them should surprise John on the day, and none of them should surprise you

00:16:22.169 --> 00:16:22.524
either.

00:16:25.074 --> 00:16:29.799
These next slides are the seven golden rules for testifying, written for the client.

00:16:30.149 --> 00:16:31.194
Start with the why.

00:16:31.544 --> 00:16:35.382
The defense is trying to get the client to contradict themselves,

00:16:35.382 --> 00:16:39.810
lose their temper, or exaggerate, because any of those damages credibility.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.399
Rule one is the foundation: always tell the truth.

00:16:43.749 --> 00:16:46.025
A case with weak spots can be saved.

00:16:46.375 --> 00:16:48.778
A case built on a lie usually can't.

00:16:49.128 --> 00:16:53.159
Prior accidents and prior injuries show up in insurance databases,

00:16:53.159 --> 00:16:57.557
and once a jury catches a plaintiff lying, the case is effectively over.

00:16:57.907 --> 00:17:02.914
For John, that means the 2021 low-back visits are simply part of the truth,

00:17:02.914 --> 00:17:03.782
told plainly.

00:17:04.132 --> 00:17:08.323
Rule two is to pause before answering, the same two seconds we talked about.

00:17:08.673 --> 00:17:11.476
It gives time to think, keeps the record clean,

00:17:11.476 --> 00:17:13.921
and gives Attorney Thorne room to object.

00:17:14.271 --> 00:17:16.302
Rule three is to answer out loud.

00:17:16.652 --> 00:17:19.433
A court reporter can't write down a nod, a shrug,

00:17:19.433 --> 00:17:22.213
or an uh-huh, and that's just as true over video.

00:17:22.563 --> 00:17:25.013
So the answer is yes or no, in words.

00:17:27.563 --> 00:17:29.165
Rule four is never guess.

00:17:29.515 --> 00:17:32.383
The key is the difference between an estimate and a guess.

00:17:32.733 --> 00:17:36.425
An estimate rests on what the client actually saw or felt.

00:17:36.775 --> 00:17:38.841
A guess claims something they couldn't know.

00:17:39.191 --> 00:17:40.213
Think about John.

00:17:40.563 --> 00:17:45.004
He was the front-seat passenger, and Robert Smith's truck came at the Escape from

00:17:45.004 --> 00:17:45.497
the side.

00:17:45.847 --> 00:17:50.503
If he's asked how fast Smith was going, he can only describe what he experienced.

00:17:50.853 --> 00:17:53.338
Claiming a number he couldn't have seen is a guess,

00:17:53.338 --> 00:17:55.044
and the defense will pick it apart.

00:17:55.394 --> 00:17:59.736
Teach clients that I don't know and I don't recall are complete, honest answers.

00:18:00.086 --> 00:18:01.886
Rule five is to keep it short.

00:18:02.236 --> 00:18:05.092
Answer just the question asked, then stop.

00:18:05.442 --> 00:18:09.686
Asked what day of the week it happened, the slide's answer is a single word,

00:18:09.686 --> 00:18:11.641
not a story about why you remember.

00:18:11.991 --> 00:18:15.393
Every extra detail is something new the defense can question.

00:18:15.743 --> 00:18:18.483
The goal is to give them the smallest target possible.

00:18:21.033 --> 00:18:25.619
Rule six should sound familiar: the silent trap, the same tactic from the drill.

00:18:25.969 --> 00:18:29.495
The defense attorney gets a short answer and then just waits,

00:18:29.495 --> 00:18:31.460
hoping the client starts rambling.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:36.211
Once the question is answered, the client sits quietly and waits for the next one.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:38.348
Rule seven is about mindset.

00:18:38.698 --> 00:18:42.779
Many clients walk in hoping to prove the other driver was a bad person,

00:18:42.779 --> 00:18:44.503
or to finally hear an apology.

00:18:44.853 --> 00:18:47.500
On our file, John might feel that strongly.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:51.310
Robert Smith was cited for failing to stop at the stop sign.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.098
But the deposition isn't the place to argue that.

00:18:54.448 --> 00:18:56.677
It's the defense gathering information.

00:18:57.027 --> 00:19:00.011
Trying to win the case there will only make John look combative.

00:19:00.361 --> 00:19:03.065
The proof of what happened lives in the evidence,

00:19:03.065 --> 00:19:05.272
and arguing it is Attorney Thorne's job.

00:19:05.622 --> 00:19:09.748
John's job is to be polite and calm, and to leave the legal battling to his

00:19:09.748 --> 00:19:10.243
attorney.

00:19:12.793 --> 00:19:17.379
Here's the client-facing version of the dress code, plus tone and body language.

00:19:17.729 --> 00:19:20.144
The reason behind it is the same as before.

00:19:20.494 --> 00:19:25.436
The insurance company uses the deposition to predict how a real jury will react to

00:19:25.436 --> 00:19:26.159
this person.

00:19:26.509 --> 00:19:29.870
On clothing, the target is business casual: clean,

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:32.558
respectful, and serious about the claim.

00:19:32.908 --> 00:19:38.214
For men, a collared shirt, slacks or neat jeans without holes, and closed-toe shoes.

00:19:38.564 --> 00:19:43.254
For women, a professional blouse, slacks, a modest dress, or a neat skirt.

00:19:43.604 --> 00:19:48.747
And leave out sweatpants, graphic tees, heavy jewelry, hats, and athletic wear.

00:19:49.097 --> 00:19:52.754
Even on a video deposition like John's, what's on camera counts.

00:19:53.104 --> 00:19:55.032
On demeanor, there are two points.

00:19:55.382 --> 00:19:56.984
First, stay calm.

00:19:57.334 --> 00:20:00.399
If the defense gets under the client's skin and they snap,

00:20:00.399 --> 00:20:04.520
the report will say they're easily rattled and will look aggressive to a jury.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:07.401
Second, describe the pain honestly.

00:20:07.751 --> 00:20:09.737
Don't downplay it, and don't inflate it.

00:20:10.087 --> 00:20:14.606
A plain, factual account of what the client can and can't do is what sounds

00:20:14.606 --> 00:20:15.149
credible.

00:20:17.698 --> 00:20:22.023
One more thing to prepare the client for, because it catches people off guard:

00:20:22.023 --> 00:20:22.633
objections.

00:20:22.983 --> 00:20:27.240
In the middle of a question, the client's attorney may suddenly say objection,

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.297
followed by a short legal term like form or speculation.

00:20:30.647 --> 00:20:33.050
Clients often think something has gone wrong.

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.294
It's routine.

00:20:34.644 --> 00:20:38.777
It's simply the lawyer putting a formal flag on the record to protect the witness.

00:20:39.127 --> 00:20:44.187
On our file, that's Attorney Thorne protecting John while Attorney Mercer asks the

00:20:44.187 --> 00:20:44.805
questions.

00:20:45.155 --> 00:20:48.998
The key instruction is to stop talking the instant the attorney speaks,

00:20:48.998 --> 00:20:49.973
even mid-sentence.

00:20:50.323 --> 00:20:51.368
Then listen.

00:20:51.718 --> 00:20:54.599
Most of the time, in about 95 percent of cases,

00:20:54.599 --> 00:20:59.566
the lawyer states the objection and then says the client can go ahead and answer.

00:20:59.916 --> 00:21:03.855
Occasionally, if a question intrudes on private medical or legal rights,

00:21:03.855 --> 00:21:06.754
the attorney will instruct the witness not to answer.

00:21:07.104 --> 00:21:10.274
Then the client stays quiet and waits for the next question.

00:21:10.624 --> 00:21:12.458
That wraps up deposition prep.

00:21:12.808 --> 00:21:15.269
Next, the documents that drive a lawsuit.

00:21:17.819 --> 00:21:19.584
Let's open the litigation toolkit.

00:21:19.934 --> 00:21:21.478
Here's the mindset shift.

00:21:21.828 --> 00:21:24.789
In pre-lit, documents are mostly something we track.

00:21:25.139 --> 00:21:29.873
In litigation, they're evidence, and a missing or misfiled one can put a trial at

00:21:29.873 --> 00:21:30.166
risk.

00:21:30.516 --> 00:21:34.644
So you need to know the five core documents well enough to spot each one in a file

00:21:34.644 --> 00:21:35.148
instantly.

00:21:35.498 --> 00:21:37.309
We'll take them one per slide.

00:21:37.659 --> 00:21:40.794
First is the Complaint, which some courts call a Petition.

00:21:41.144 --> 00:21:42.433
The attorney drafts it.

00:21:42.782 --> 00:21:46.266
It tells what happened, says the defendant was negligent,

00:21:46.266 --> 00:21:48.100
and formally asks for damages.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:52.107
On our file, Attorney Thorne drafted and approved the complaint.

00:21:52.457 --> 00:21:57.458
On September 22nd, 2026, you tagged the Litigation Desk paralegal,

00:21:57.458 --> 00:22:01.247
Nadia Brooks, to prepare the filing set around it,

00:22:01.247 --> 00:22:04.960
Attorney Thorne signed the set on September 23rd,

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:11.174
and it was e-filed on September 24th, 2026, in the State Court of Riverton County,

00:22:11.174 --> 00:22:13.599
Civil Action Number 26-CV-04417.

00:22:13.949 --> 00:22:16.050
Why do you care about that filing date?

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:20.600
Because it's the kickoff of the whole lawsuit, and it's the date that proves we beat

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:21.950
the Statute of Limitations.

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:28.610
Second is the Answer, the defense's formal response to our Complaint.

00:22:28.959 --> 00:22:31.003
Don't be surprised by what's in it.

00:22:31.353 --> 00:22:36.279
The defense will almost always deny liability and list affirmative defenses,

00:22:36.279 --> 00:22:38.806
often claiming our client was at fault.

00:22:39.156 --> 00:22:44.683
Our file shows exactly that, and this is the liability dispute from today's agenda.

00:22:45.033 --> 00:22:50.838
On November 12th, 2026, Attorney Grant Mercer filed Robert Smith's Answer.

00:22:51.188 --> 00:22:53.812
It denies the claim and pleads three defenses.

00:22:54.162 --> 00:22:58.448
Comparative negligence: the Escape entered the all-way stop out of turn,

00:22:58.448 --> 00:22:59.758
and John was unbelted.

00:23:00.108 --> 00:23:04.670
Causation: the gap in treatment, and an MRI the defense calls degenerative.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:08.399
And failure to mitigate: the disruption in John's care.

00:23:08.749 --> 00:23:10.571
Read the Answer as a road map.

00:23:10.921 --> 00:23:15.391
It tells you what the defense will fight about, so you know what evidence to gather.

00:23:15.741 --> 00:23:20.393
Here, the file already points the way: the doorbell video showing the Escape stopped

00:23:20.393 --> 00:23:24.768
and Smith rolling through, the Escape's event data recorder showing John's belt

00:23:24.768 --> 00:23:29.309
buckled, and the MRI reading of no significant degenerative change at the adjacent

00:23:29.309 --> 00:23:29.696
levels.

00:23:32.246 --> 00:23:36.461
Third are Interrogatories, and you've already met them in the discovery phase.

00:23:36.811 --> 00:23:39.898
They're written questions from one side to the other,

00:23:39.898 --> 00:23:43.452
answered under oath, with a strict deadline, usually 30 days.

00:23:43.802 --> 00:23:46.575
That's why the slide calls them the deadline clock,

00:23:46.575 --> 00:23:48.968
and the clock starts the moment they arrive.

00:23:49.318 --> 00:23:55.508
On our file, Robert Smith's defense served 25 interrogatories on John on December

00:23:55.508 --> 00:23:56.272
1st, 2026.

00:23:56.622 --> 00:24:01.986
Because they came by mail, the responses are due on January 4th, 2027.

00:24:02.336 --> 00:24:05.819
We'll walk through that math when we get to deadline calculations.

00:24:06.169 --> 00:24:08.630
So the right move isn't to set the set aside for later.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:13.266
As soon as it arrives, get John scheduled to work through the answers in a Discovery

00:24:13.266 --> 00:24:14.031
Intake Meeting.

00:24:14.381 --> 00:24:18.061
The earlier you start, the more time there is to get the answers right.

00:24:20.611 --> 00:24:22.701
Fourth are Requests for Production.

00:24:23.051 --> 00:24:26.886
Where interrogatories ask questions, these ask for things:

00:24:26.886 --> 00:24:31.317
medical records, payroll logs, photos, even social media downloads.

00:24:31.667 --> 00:24:33.699
This one lands squarely on you.

00:24:34.049 --> 00:24:37.195
You're responsible for gathering every single item requested.

00:24:37.545 --> 00:24:41.458
On our file, the same December 1st packet carried 30 of them.

00:24:41.808 --> 00:24:45.652
In practice, that means working through the requests line by line,

00:24:45.652 --> 00:24:49.089
checking what the file already holds from the pre-lit work,

00:24:49.089 --> 00:24:52.001
and chasing down the rest, so nothing gets missed.

00:24:52.351 --> 00:24:55.799
And remember, these documents become evidence in the case.

00:24:56.149 --> 00:25:00.945
What we hand over, and how completely, matters just as much as the client's written

00:25:00.945 --> 00:25:03.429
answers, and it's all due on the same date.

00:25:05.979 --> 00:25:11.226
The fifth document is the one to fear the most: Requests for Admission, or RFAs.

00:25:11.576 --> 00:25:15.019
These are statements the other side has to either admit or deny,

00:25:15.019 --> 00:25:17.439
something like, admit that you were speeding.

00:25:17.789 --> 00:25:18.881
Here's the danger.

00:25:19.231 --> 00:25:23.064
If RFAs aren't answered by the deadline, they can be deemed admitted,

00:25:23.064 --> 00:25:25.175
meaning the court treats them as true.

00:25:25.525 --> 00:25:33.315
Our file has 15 of them, served December 1st, 2026, and due January 4th, 2027.

00:25:33.665 --> 00:25:36.310
Now think about the defenses in Smith's Answer,

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:38.449
like the claim that John was unbelted.

00:25:38.799 --> 00:25:41.443
If a statement along those lines went unanswered,

00:25:41.443 --> 00:25:45.707
John could be treated as having admitted it simply because a response was late.

00:25:46.057 --> 00:25:47.322
That can end a case.

00:25:47.672 --> 00:25:51.306
It's why RFAs get special priority in the KPIs coming up.

00:25:51.656 --> 00:25:54.152
When you see one, treat it as urgent.

00:25:56.702 --> 00:26:00.510
Now that you know the documents, let's look at the processes you'll run every week.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:04.459
Court procedures are rigid, so these have to become routine.

00:26:04.809 --> 00:26:07.247
Process one is service of process.

00:26:07.597 --> 00:26:11.138
Filing means nothing until the defendant is legally notified,

00:26:11.138 --> 00:26:13.344
so you manage service like a pipeline.

00:26:13.694 --> 00:26:18.142
The flowchart shows the steps: get the summons issued and signed by the clerk,

00:26:18.142 --> 00:26:22.931
assign a process server and send the papers, follow up every few days while watching

00:26:22.931 --> 00:26:27.550
for a defendant who's dodging, and file proof of service once the affidavit comes

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:27.835
back.

00:26:28.185 --> 00:26:33.838
On our file, the clerk issued the summons when the complaint was filed on September

00:26:33.838 --> 00:26:39.082
24th, Allied Process Services took the papers, and the affidavit was filed on

00:26:39.082 --> 00:26:40.375
October 16th, 2026.

00:26:40.725 --> 00:26:43.523
As you'll see shortly, Smith didn't make it easy.

00:26:43.873 --> 00:26:45.522
Process two is docketing.

00:26:45.872 --> 00:26:50.144
Every filing in the case, from either side, creates a docket entry.

00:26:50.494 --> 00:26:54.094
Check the docket yourself every week, and don't rely only on email.

00:26:54.444 --> 00:26:57.482
If a Motion to Dismiss notice lands in your spam folder,

00:26:57.482 --> 00:26:59.598
the clock is still running against you.

00:27:02.148 --> 00:27:05.248
The third weekly process is subpoenas to third parties.

00:27:05.598 --> 00:27:10.541
Sometimes a hospital, a phone company, or an employer simply won't release records

00:27:10.541 --> 00:27:11.264
voluntarily.

00:27:11.614 --> 00:27:14.342
A subpoena gives us the power to require it.

00:27:14.692 --> 00:27:16.201
The flow has four steps.

00:27:16.551 --> 00:27:20.703
First, you draft the court-approved form, the Subpoena Duces Tecum,

00:27:20.703 --> 00:27:22.623
a command to produce documents.

00:27:22.973 --> 00:27:27.490
Second, before it goes to the third party, a copy goes to the defense attorney.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:32.112
Most states require advance notice, typically 5 to 10 days.

00:27:32.462 --> 00:27:35.678
On our file, that copy would go to Attorney Mercer.

00:27:36.028 --> 00:27:40.568
Third, a process server formally delivers it to the facility's Registered Agent.

00:27:40.917 --> 00:27:43.715
And fourth, don't just wait for the records.

00:27:44.065 --> 00:27:48.353
Call the facility's legal or records department about 5 days before the deadline to

00:27:48.353 --> 00:27:51.194
make sure the records are really being pulled together.

00:27:51.544 --> 00:27:56.017
That follow-up call is often what makes the difference between records that arrive

00:27:56.017 --> 00:27:57.871
on time and a deadline that slips.

00:28:00.421 --> 00:28:04.966
Good litigation work depends on a clean file, and these three rules keep it that

00:28:04.966 --> 00:28:05.193
way.

00:28:05.543 --> 00:28:09.676
Rule one: once suit is filed, keep pre-lit and lit separate.

00:28:10.026 --> 00:28:13.404
Give the litigation side its own folders for pleadings,

00:28:13.404 --> 00:28:16.597
discovery sent and received, motions, and subpoenas.

00:28:16.947 --> 00:28:21.178
On our file, that split belongs at the moment the status changed to Litigation,

00:28:21.178 --> 00:28:25.516
so months of pre-lit letters with Keystone never get mixed in with court filings.

00:28:25.866 --> 00:28:28.803
Rule two: treat the Scheduling Order like the Bible.

00:28:29.153 --> 00:28:34.737
Ours was entered on December 10th, 2026, at the Case Management Conference.

00:28:35.087 --> 00:28:39.440
It sets the plaintiff's expert disclosure for March 15th,

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:44.863
2027, mediation completed by March 31st, discovery closing on May 28th,

00:28:44.863 --> 00:28:48.299
and a trial calendar of September 13th, 2027.

00:28:48.649 --> 00:28:53.062
Print it, put it on the front of the file, and enter every date on the master

00:28:53.062 --> 00:28:55.871
calendar with warnings at 30, 14, and 7 days out.

00:28:56.221 --> 00:28:58.868
Rule three: get every extension in writing.

00:28:59.218 --> 00:29:02.683
If your attorney agrees on a call to give the defense another week,

00:29:02.683 --> 00:29:05.011
a phone agreement means nothing to the court.

00:29:05.361 --> 00:29:07.927
Send a short confirming email with the new date.

00:29:10.477 --> 00:29:14.111
Here's how you'll measure your document management, with three habits.

00:29:14.461 --> 00:29:16.945
First, the 48-hour docket rule.

00:29:17.295 --> 00:29:21.894
Anything that comes in from the court or opposing counsel gets saved into our system

00:29:21.894 --> 00:29:26.328
and put on the calendar within 48 hours, so no deadline sits unnoticed in a pile.

00:29:26.678 --> 00:29:29.929
Think of the discovery packet that arrived on our file in December.

00:29:30.279 --> 00:29:33.854
It carried a response date that could cost John the case if it slipped.

00:29:34.204 --> 00:29:39.452
Second, treat Requests for Admission like a structural fire, and you just saw why.

00:29:39.802 --> 00:29:44.233
The moment an RFA lands on your desk, the assigned attorney hears about it,

00:29:44.233 --> 00:29:45.119
that same hour.

00:29:45.469 --> 00:29:49.336
On our file, that's Attorney Thorne, and those 15 RFAs.

00:29:49.686 --> 00:29:53.319
Third, review every outstanding subpoena every 10 days.

00:29:53.669 --> 00:29:57.141
That's your built-in check that third parties are actually complying.

00:29:57.491 --> 00:30:02.057
Together, these three habits turn the rules we just covered into a routine you can

00:30:02.057 --> 00:30:03.226
run without thinking.

00:30:05.776 --> 00:30:07.518
Now we go one level deeper.

00:30:07.868 --> 00:30:10.201
It's not enough to know a document's name.

00:30:10.551 --> 00:30:14.920
A strong litigation case manager knows how each one is built and what can go wrong

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:15.346
with it.

00:30:15.696 --> 00:30:17.437
So let's take the Complaint apart.

00:30:17.787 --> 00:30:22.425
The attorney handles the legal arguments, and you make sure the facts underneath are

00:30:22.425 --> 00:30:22.757
solid.

00:30:23.107 --> 00:30:25.045
The table shows five sections.

00:30:25.395 --> 00:30:29.422
The caption names the court, the parties, and the case number,

00:30:29.422 --> 00:30:32.735
which for us is the State Court of Riverton County,

00:30:32.735 --> 00:30:36.762
John Doe versus Robert Smith, Civil Action Number 26-CV-04417.

00:30:37.112 --> 00:30:40.258
Jurisdiction explains why the court can decide the case.

00:30:40.608 --> 00:30:43.673
The factual allegations tell the story of the crash.

00:30:44.023 --> 00:30:46.461
The causes of action name the legal claims.

00:30:46.811 --> 00:30:49.876
And the prayer for relief is the demand for compensation.

00:30:50.226 --> 00:30:53.012
Before it goes for signature, run three checks.

00:30:53.362 --> 00:30:57.101
Is the filing date safely inside the Statute of Limitations?

00:30:57.451 --> 00:31:01.762
Is the defendant's legal name verified through the Secretary of State or property

00:31:01.762 --> 00:31:02.188
records?

00:31:02.537 --> 00:31:08.624
In John Doe versus Robert Smith, for example, is the defendant Robert Smith himself,

00:31:08.624 --> 00:31:12.174
or a business like Robert Smith Enterprises, LLC?

00:31:12.524 --> 00:31:14.788
Getting that wrong is a costly mistake.

00:31:15.138 --> 00:31:19.029
And if the defendant is a business, have you found their Registered Agent for

00:31:19.029 --> 00:31:19.433
service?

00:31:21.983 --> 00:31:25.083
Let's revisit written discovery, from both directions.

00:31:25.433 --> 00:31:29.740
When a discovery packet arrives from the defense, it can't sit in the inbox.

00:31:30.090 --> 00:31:32.656
It gets broken down and calendared right away.

00:31:33.006 --> 00:31:35.340
The flow shows two things happening at once.

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:39.102
You calculate the due date, typically 30 days from service,

00:31:39.102 --> 00:31:43.266
and you pull the client file to see what we already gathered in pre-lit,

00:31:43.266 --> 00:31:44.885
so we don't start from zero.

00:31:45.235 --> 00:31:48.563
Then you bring the client in for a discovery intake conference,

00:31:48.563 --> 00:31:51.098
about an hour, to answer the questions together.

00:31:51.448 --> 00:31:54.223
That's the path John's December 1st packet takes.

00:31:54.573 --> 00:31:55.768
Now flip it around.

00:31:56.118 --> 00:32:01.323
On our file, we served our own discovery on Smith on December 14th,

00:32:01.323 --> 00:32:07.537
2026, by email, with both sides' consent, so his responses are due January 13th,

00:32:07.537 --> 00:32:07.926
2027.

00:32:08.276 --> 00:32:12.989
When we draft our own interrogatories, the slide names three baseline topics.

00:32:13.339 --> 00:32:16.668
Insurance coverage, including excess and umbrella limits,

00:32:16.668 --> 00:32:19.121
so we know what money is really available.

00:32:19.471 --> 00:32:23.219
Whether the defendant was working or running an errand for an employer at the time

00:32:23.219 --> 00:32:23.813
of the crash.

00:32:24.163 --> 00:32:27.693
And whether they've hired an investigator to follow or film our client.

00:32:30.243 --> 00:32:34.503
This slide is about something that sounds simple but isn't: counting days.

00:32:34.853 --> 00:32:39.227
In litigation, being one day late can get a case dismissed with prejudice,

00:32:39.227 --> 00:32:41.355
meaning it can never be filed again.

00:32:41.705 --> 00:32:45.580
The general rule is that you don't count the day of the event that starts the clock,

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:46.964
but you do count the last day.

00:32:47.314 --> 00:32:49.125
Then watch for the mailbox trap.

00:32:49.475 --> 00:32:52.949
When something is served by mail or electronic portal,

00:32:52.949 --> 00:32:56.743
many states add 3 days, so always confirm your local rules.

00:32:57.093 --> 00:33:00.720
The matrix shows the chain: filing starts the clock on service,

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:05.325
service starts the clock on the Answer, discovery starts the clock on responses,

00:33:05.325 --> 00:33:08.088
and a motion starts the clock on the opposition.

00:33:08.438 --> 00:33:09.982
The ranges vary by court.

00:33:10.332 --> 00:33:13.258
Here's how our file's configured dates work out.

00:33:13.608 --> 00:33:22.234
Filed September 24th, 2026, with service due within 120 days, by January 22nd, 2027.

00:33:22.584 --> 00:33:27.890
Smith served October 14th, Answer due November 13th, 2026.

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:34.038
And discovery mailed December 1st: 30 days plus 3 for mail lands on Sunday,

00:33:34.038 --> 00:33:38.523
January 3rd, so the responses are due Monday, January 4th,

00:33:38.523 --> 00:33:38.909
2027.

00:33:41.459 --> 00:33:44.222
What happens when a defendant doesn't want to be found?

00:33:44.572 --> 00:33:45.548
It's common.

00:33:45.897 --> 00:33:51.087
Some people hide, stop answering the door, or even quit their jobs to avoid service.

00:33:51.437 --> 00:33:54.152
And if they aren't served by the court's deadline,

00:33:54.152 --> 00:33:57.138
the judge can dismiss the case for Want of Prosecution.

00:33:57.488 --> 00:33:59.438
Our file is the example.

00:33:59.788 --> 00:34:05.693
Allied Process Services tried Robert Smith at the address on the police report,

00:34:05.693 --> 00:34:09.206
118 Larkspur Lane, on October 1st, 5th and 8th,

00:34:09.206 --> 00:34:11.897
2026, and all three attempts failed.

00:34:12.247 --> 00:34:14.476
A neighbor said he had moved in August.

00:34:14.826 --> 00:34:16.463
So the protocol kicked in.

00:34:16.813 --> 00:34:21.510
Step one on the flowchart is a skip trace, a background search to find where someone

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:22.572
actually lives now.

00:34:22.922 --> 00:34:28.521
TraceLine Investigations ran it on October 9th and found Smith at 4410 Quarry Ridge

00:34:28.521 --> 00:34:29.667
Court, Millbrook.

00:34:30.017 --> 00:34:34.557
Allied served him personally on October 14th, 2026.

00:34:34.907 --> 00:34:38.657
Because the skip trace worked, we never needed the later steps:

00:34:38.657 --> 00:34:42.586
varying the server's hours to early morning, evening and weekends,

00:34:42.586 --> 00:34:46.039
and finally asking the court to allow alternative service,

00:34:46.039 --> 00:34:48.897
like social media, posting on the door, or mail.

00:34:51.447 --> 00:34:54.470
Let's take a closer look at the Subpoena Duces Tecum,

00:34:54.470 --> 00:34:58.064
our tool for making reluctant third parties hand over evidence.

00:34:58.414 --> 00:35:00.887
The biggest mistake is asking for too much.

00:35:01.237 --> 00:35:05.077
A subpoena demanding all records of any kind invites a Motion to Quash,

00:35:05.077 --> 00:35:07.890
with the other side calling it a fishing expedition.

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:13.383
The fix is to be narrow: exactly which records, about which person, for which dates.

00:35:13.733 --> 00:35:17.559
The slide's sample wording, limited to emergency room records,

00:35:17.559 --> 00:35:20.767
triage notes and imaging for the accident date only,

00:35:20.767 --> 00:35:21.570
is the model.

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:28.479
On our file, the accident date in a request like that would be February 6th, 2026.

00:35:28.829 --> 00:35:31.650
The workflow ties back to what we covered earlier.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:35.309
Draft the form with the precise list attached as Exhibit A.

00:35:35.659 --> 00:35:38.887
Give the defense its copy before the third party gets theirs.

00:35:39.237 --> 00:35:43.333
Have the process server personally deliver it to the records custodian or Registered

00:35:43.333 --> 00:35:43.625
Agent.

00:35:43.975 --> 00:35:45.972
And don't forget the invoice check.

00:35:46.322 --> 00:35:51.677
Facilities charge for copies, so track, pay and process those invoices right away to

00:35:51.677 --> 00:35:53.207
keep the request moving.

00:35:55.757 --> 00:35:58.427
Remember the rule about separating pre-lit from lit?

00:35:58.777 --> 00:36:00.983
This slide shows what that looks like.

00:36:01.333 --> 00:36:02.738
The point is uniformity.

00:36:03.088 --> 00:36:06.815
If every litigation file across the firm is set up the same way,

00:36:06.815 --> 00:36:10.309
anyone can open any file and find what they need in seconds.

00:36:10.659 --> 00:36:13.852
And this structure goes in place the moment a lawsuit is filed.

00:36:14.202 --> 00:36:19.008
The master folder carries the client's name, so for us, Doe, John.

00:36:19.358 --> 00:36:22.934
Under it are five sub-folders that follow the life of the case.

00:36:23.284 --> 00:36:26.153
Pleadings holds the filed Complaint, the summons,

00:36:26.153 --> 00:36:28.729
and Smith's Answer and affirmative defenses.

00:36:29.079 --> 00:36:32.249
Service documents holds Allied's Affidavit of Service.

00:36:32.599 --> 00:36:36.494
Then discovery gets two folders, one for what we sent the defense,

00:36:36.494 --> 00:36:39.918
like our December 14th set, and one for what they sent us,

00:36:39.918 --> 00:36:43.872
like the December 1st packet, along with John's verified responses.

00:36:44.222 --> 00:36:47.519
And subpoenas holds each subpoena with its proof of service.

00:36:47.869 --> 00:36:50.865
Notice how the folders mirror the processes we've discussed.

00:36:51.215 --> 00:36:54.988
File as you go, and the structure does a lot of the organizing for you.

00:36:57.538 --> 00:37:00.961
Let's wrap up the litigation section with three advanced KPIs,

00:37:00.961 --> 00:37:05.212
the standards that separate a solid litigation case manager from a great one.

00:37:05.562 --> 00:37:07.234
The first is zero defaults.

00:37:07.584 --> 00:37:12.373
No discovery or motion deadline ever passes without either a filed response or a

00:37:12.373 --> 00:37:15.246
signed, written extension from opposing counsel.

00:37:15.596 --> 00:37:21.194
On our file, that means January 4th, 2027 is not negotiable unless Attorney Mercer

00:37:21.194 --> 00:37:23.514
signs off on more time in writing.

00:37:23.864 --> 00:37:26.546
The second is the 14-day service milestone.

00:37:26.896 --> 00:37:31.489
If a defendant still hasn't been served 14 days after the process server received

00:37:31.489 --> 00:37:33.247
the packet, don't keep waiting.

00:37:33.597 --> 00:37:36.128
Run a skip trace and update your attorney.

00:37:36.478 --> 00:37:39.473
That's the same move that found Robert Smith in Millbrook.

00:37:39.823 --> 00:37:41.925
The third is the clean discovery check.

00:37:42.275 --> 00:37:46.461
Before a draft response goes to your attorney, every blank is filled in and a

00:37:46.461 --> 00:37:48.962
signature-ready verification page is attached.

00:37:49.312 --> 00:37:53.840
Your attorney's review time should go to substance, not to chasing missing pieces.

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:59.884
Now the second part of today, property damage in litigation.

00:38:00.234 --> 00:38:03.057
When someone's negligence damages a house or a car,

00:38:03.057 --> 00:38:06.155
the law tries to make the owner whole again financially.

00:38:06.505 --> 00:38:07.922
That's the key idea.

00:38:08.272 --> 00:38:09.700
The goal isn't a windfall.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:13.057
It's putting the person back where they were before the incident.

00:38:13.407 --> 00:38:16.402
The table sorts recoverable damages into three kinds.

00:38:16.752 --> 00:38:21.151
Economic, or special, damages are losses you can put a number on,

00:38:21.151 --> 00:38:25.889
like medical bills, lost wages, lost earning capacity and rehab costs.

00:38:26.239 --> 00:38:30.040
Non-economic, or general, damages are real but harder to measure,

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:34.017
like pain and suffering, emotional distress, and loss of consortium,

00:38:34.017 --> 00:38:35.655
the strain on relationships.

00:38:36.005 --> 00:38:41.179
And punitive damages exist to punish especially reckless or intentional conduct and

00:38:41.179 --> 00:38:41.740
deter it.

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.274
Now our file.

00:38:43.624 --> 00:38:46.673
John's suit pleads medical specials, lost wages,

00:38:46.673 --> 00:38:49.151
future medical, and pain and suffering.

00:38:49.501 --> 00:38:53.274
But no property damage is pleaded, because John owns no car.

00:38:53.624 --> 00:38:57.117
The damaged car is his mother Margaret's 2019 Honda Civic,

00:38:57.117 --> 00:39:00.369
parked at the curb when the Escape was pushed into it.

00:39:00.719 --> 00:39:04.683
That's her own claim with Keystone, separate from John's lawsuit,

00:39:04.683 --> 00:39:06.756
and it's the one we'll value next.

00:39:09.306 --> 00:39:11.280
Knowing the categories is one thing.

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:15.496
The real fight in a property damage case is usually over the number.

00:39:15.846 --> 00:39:19.341
Courts use set formulas, and the first is the lesser-of rule.

00:39:19.691 --> 00:39:22.630
For personal property like a car, compare two figures:

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:27.038
the cost to repair, and the drop in value, meaning what it was worth before minus

00:39:27.038 --> 00:39:28.236
what it's worth after.

00:39:28.586 --> 00:39:31.140
The award is usually the smaller of the two.

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:35.949
The slide's example is a general one: a car worth five thousand dollars with seven

00:39:35.949 --> 00:39:40.081
thousand dollars in repairs gets capped at its value and treated as totaled.

00:39:40.431 --> 00:39:43.926
On Margaret's Civic, the question was repair or total.

00:39:44.276 --> 00:39:47.538
Keystone valued it at 17,600 dollars.

00:39:47.888 --> 00:39:52.071
The final repair, including a structural supplement,

00:39:52.071 --> 00:39:56.816
came to 9,255 dollars, which is 52.6 percent of that value.

00:39:57.166 --> 00:40:00.945
Our file uses 75 percent of value as the total-loss line,

00:40:00.945 --> 00:40:06.315
so the Civic was repairable, not a total loss, and Keystone paid the shop for the

00:40:06.315 --> 00:40:06.779
repair.

00:40:07.129 --> 00:40:11.286
That's the make-whole principle at work: the owner is restored, not enriched.

00:40:13.836 --> 00:40:17.620
What about something that means far more to the owner than its market price?

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:22.689
Standard law looks at fair market value, so sentimental value usually doesn't move

00:40:22.689 --> 00:40:23.323
the number.

00:40:23.673 --> 00:40:25.124
There's a narrow exception.

00:40:25.474 --> 00:40:29.317
Some jurisdictions allow extra recovery when an item has a unique,

00:40:29.317 --> 00:40:34.208
special value to its owner, but you have to show the defendant knew about that value

00:40:34.208 --> 00:40:36.770
before the incident, or acted intentionally.

00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:40.534
On Margaret's Civic, the open fight isn't sentimental value.

00:40:40.884 --> 00:40:42.381
It's two other lines.

00:40:42.731 --> 00:40:47.886
Loss of Use, 2,016 dollars for the days she had no car and no paid rental,

00:40:47.886 --> 00:40:49.558
which Keystone disputes.

00:40:49.908 --> 00:40:55.145
And Diminished Value, the value a car still loses after a proper repair:

00:40:55.145 --> 00:41:01.110
an independent appraisal says 3,600 dollars, and Keystone's 17c formula offers 792

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:01.692
dollars.

00:41:02.042 --> 00:41:05.672
Remember, this is Margaret's claim, not part of John's suit,

00:41:05.672 --> 00:41:07.487
and its outcome is still open.

00:41:07.837 --> 00:41:11.274
Whatever you'd propose to resolve it needs Attorney Thorne's authority.

00:41:13.824 --> 00:41:16.227
That brings us to the end of Day 5.

00:41:16.577 --> 00:41:19.235
Today we took John Doe's case into litigation.

00:41:19.585 --> 00:41:22.165
We saw how the rules change once suit is filed,

00:41:22.165 --> 00:41:26.610
how Robert Smith was found and served, and how the Answer turned into a liability

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:29.024
dispute over the stop sign and the seatbelt.

00:41:29.374 --> 00:41:33.927
We prepared for John's deposition, including the stretch when he went missing,

00:41:33.927 --> 00:41:36.963
and built your toolkit of core documents, processes,

00:41:36.963 --> 00:41:37.780
and deadlines.

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:41.485
Then we saw how the property damage on Margaret's Civic is valued.

00:41:41.835 --> 00:41:45.547
If there's one idea to take away, it's this: in litigation,

00:41:45.547 --> 00:41:47.245
the calendar is everything.

00:41:47.595 --> 00:41:50.373
Review your notes on John Doe versus Robert Smith,

00:41:50.373 --> 00:41:52.263
and thank you for your work today.

